<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Republic of Korea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> (the Republic of Korea) is a state in the southern part of the Korean peninsula, established in 1948. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> of 1950–1953 it grew under authoritarian governments, above all that of Park Chung-hee, into one of the world's leading industrial economies, and it became a democracy in 1987. Its export-led development was a model for other Asian countries, including Malaysia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/look-east-policy">Look East Policy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-korea.jpg" alt="South Korea" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Government of the Republic of Korea Vector: Great Brightstar and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Korea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Korean Peninsula was inhabited as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s founding mythology, the history of Korea begins with the founding of Gojoseon in 2333 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dangun">Dangun</a>; Chinese records note Gojoseon in the early 7th century BCE. In 108 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> defeated Wiman Joseon and installed four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> in the north of the peninsula. The Lelang commandery remained a centre of cultural and economic exchange for four centuries, until Goguryeo conquered it in 313 CE.</p><p>From the Proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> states of Buyeo, Okjeo, Dongye and Samhan emerged the Three Kingdoms of Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla. Goguryeo was the largest and a highly militaristic state, with 700 years of history. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a> and his son Jangsu it subdued Baekje and Silla. In the Goguryeo-Sui War it is said to have defeated a force of over a million men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Goryeo dynasty (918 to 1392) achieved lasting unification, and the Joseon dynasty (1392 to 1897) followed it, maintaining an isolationist policy from the mid-17th century. The Korean Empire (1897 to 1910) was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> in 1910. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was divided into a Soviet-occupied northern zone and a United States-occupied southern zone, and the Republic of Korea was established in August 1948.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> began in 1950 with a North Korean invasion and ended in 1953 with an armistice, with three million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> dead and both economies in ruins. No peace treaty was signed. South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> then endured a series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> punctuated by coups, revolutions and violent uprisings, while its economy grew rapidly and it became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-asian-tigers">Four Asian Tigers</a>. The June Democratic Struggle of 1987 ended authoritarian rule and led to the Sixth Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the 1987 constitution South Korea is a unitary presidential republic with a unicameral National Assembly. It is divided into ten provinces, three special self-governing provinces and several cities with special status. Its recorded economic growth was among the fastest between the early 1960s and the late 1990s; South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> call this the Miracle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>.</p><p>The population was roughly 51.7 million in 2022. South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> shares its traditional culture with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, though the two have developed distinct contemporary cultures since 1945. It has 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Korea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China, the DPRK and the Soviet Union gave aid and troops; against them the United States, Australia, the <b>Republic of Korea</b>, Thailand and New Zealand supported the South.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its culture travels too: Georgian polyphony, the kvevri winemaking method listed by UNESCO in 2013, the 1.8-million-year-old Dmanisi hominins, the oldest in Eurasia, and a diplomatic campaign that has persuaded Israel, Japan, <b>South Korea</b>, Hungary and Lithuania to drop the Russian name 'Gruzia'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Officers who had served Japan, among them Park Chung Hee, went on to careers in post-colonial <b>South Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>American combat troops arrived in 1965, and during the Tet Offensive of February 1968 over a million South Vietnamese soldiers served beside 500,000 US soldiers and 100,000 from allies such as <b>South Korea</b>, Australia and Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Mohammed bin Salman it has leaned towards China and Russia, coordinated oil cuts with Moscow, and sought defence ties with Pakistan, India and <b>South Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Mirziyoyev relations with Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Afghanistan improved drastically, and ties with China, Turkey, Russia, India, <b>South Korea</b>, Japan, the UAE and the West have grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
